[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-84467"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/assets/75df54dd30da590814e89153396ac7cf8a713fc9a6e04d14786780147ba57cfe.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(5,-1),$ and passes through the point $(17,11).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(5,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-5=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(17,11),$ we can substitute $x=17$ and $y=11$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}12=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 12=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =2pt \\\\ 6 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{6}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =p{(\\frac{6}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 12 & =p\\cdot \\frac{36}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{36}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{36}{12} \\\\ p & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-5=(3){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(3)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-5=3{t}^{2},\\,y+1=6t.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-84855/selection/e0e5d31d656c85d67103e918274c80ae55171cd59c2aa20eb1181af8b397389c" :answer/id #uuid "01ca4d8d-f5bd-5d8c-bcd6-1a21ad35a1b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}(y-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84855/selection/eef07d2482e799f8bc3f62b7b2f957bf5c9f47998f891564c82e29ba16a5e223" :answer/id #uuid "05d47e87-8d94-5722-bcc2-821195510438" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}(y+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84855/selection/c39f7530b4c208a2e220c41e49388fdcfaa6588f603dcde3c72f87dfd1dde917" :answer/id #uuid "decc5de0-73b9-5ba6-813f-b8ee87a392e3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-12(y-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84855/selection/2e7a524f9e1522f212d865d2c8da665f9430e5f5347cda8a16b242f211122822" :answer/id #uuid "e78fc381-1d46-548b-81d4-3c22b2a741b1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x+3)^{2}=12(y+1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84855/selection/d3de4e3bc165241db7b3eacff2cdc1775a977bc74459b4c642de579f2ebc2949" :answer/id #uuid "4dbffa8e-abed-5a51-a13a-d1d32514a346" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-6(y-1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-84855/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "a9acdab2-5744-579e-baad-36edcbaf391e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-84855/selection/c39f7530b4c208a2e220c41e49388fdcfaa6588f603dcde3c72f87dfd1dde917" :answer-field/choices ["q-84855/selection/e0e5d31d656c85d67103e918274c80ae55171cd59c2aa20eb1181af8b397389c" "q-84855/selection/eef07d2482e799f8bc3f62b7b2f957bf5c9f47998f891564c82e29ba16a5e223" "q-84855/selection/c39f7530b4c208a2e220c41e49388fdcfaa6588f603dcde3c72f87dfd1dde917" "q-84855/selection/2e7a524f9e1522f212d865d2c8da665f9430e5f5347cda8a16b242f211122822" "q-84855/selection/d3de4e3bc165241db7b3eacff2cdc1775a977bc74459b4c642de579f2ebc2949"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-84855" :question/id #uuid "47d76f1d-d242-5bf6-8664-6b58d2510ca1" :question/math-academy-id "q-84855" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-3=-6t,\\,y-1=-3{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x-3}{6},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x-3}{6})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-1}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{36} & =-\\frac{y-1}{3} \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =36\\cdot (-\\frac{y-1}{3}) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =-12(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-84855/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-84855"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-106462"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14056541/assets/6cd9c6a97d450761c01a9adb723985acbf4e5dc91e9877d7bd46c95db66d8c30.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-321592/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "29acce41-c002-5d4b-8e0c-899e03b85800" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321592/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "65ae4835-b693-58ed-ba98-152de30a9ad6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321592/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer-field/choices ["q-321592/field-1/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321592/field-2/053742ada9106851a4c09eb4113ea07f8a1d4519b2532614ff7f34b73ceb065e" :answer/id #uuid "52a2a18b-c37c-5810-9b54-40770c679e30" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25(y-2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-321592/field-2" :answer-field/id #uuid "444f7d45-5653-53bd-904d-da9149a5e623" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-321592/field-2/053742ada9106851a4c09eb4113ea07f8a1d4519b2532614ff7f34b73ceb065e" :answer-field/choices ["q-321592/field-2/053742ada9106851a4c09eb4113ea07f8a1d4519b2532614ff7f34b73ceb065e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-321592" :question/id #uuid "4b38783e-1aa6-5722-8348-08e33fa245db" :question/math-academy-id "q-321592" :question/problem "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola \n\n$$\nx+2=5t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+2}{5},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+2}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+2)^{2}}{25} & =y-2 \\\\ {(x+2)}^{2} & =25(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-321592/field-1" "q-321592/field-2"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14079b03-66c0-5657-8909-a7d5ea35bfc0"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-321592"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
